## What happened

**DSA has been obsolete in .NET since the `SYSLIB0039` warning was introduced (starting around .NET 7).** Apple's security frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto) do not support DSA at all, so `DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS. In .NET 10, the managed/OpenSSL fallback still worked, but .NET 11 completed the removal of that fallback path on Apple platforms.

## How to fix it

You have three options, depending on your constraints:

### Option 1 – Migrate away from DSA (recommended)
DSA is a legacy algorithm. If you control the signing side, re-sign with **ECDSA** or **RSA-PSS** and update the verifier.

### Option 2 – Use BouncyCastle for verification (if you must keep DSA)
```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(
    byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)
        PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```
This is pure managed code—works on all platforms with no native dependency.

### Option 3 – Install OpenSSL on macOS and P/Invoke into it
Install via `brew install openssl` and use `SafeEvpPKeyHandle.OpenPrivateKeyFromEngine` or raw P/Invoke. This is fragile and not recommended for production.

**Bottom line:** If the signatures are truly legacy and read-only, BouncyCastle (Option 2) is the lowest-friction fix. Long-term, migrate to ECDSA.
